ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is AWS's Docker registry.

Exercise 2:
Reflection questions
Q1. `docker tag` doesn't create a new copy of the image, it adds a second name pointing at the same image layers. Why does the ECR repository URI need to be baked into the tag itself, rather than just passing the registry as a separate argument to `docker push`?
Answer: `docker push` has no separate 'registry' argument, so the registry cannot be passed. `docker push` synthax is <image>:<tag>. Docker determines where to push purely by parsing the registry hostname out of the image name itself. So, the image name is the address that specifies destination, as a result the registry URI has to be baked directly into the tag or Docker has no way to know where to go.
Q2. Your Docker image also bundles PyTorch and ESM-2 weights, likely several GB. What's one practical consequence of that size when it comes to push time and ECR storage costs, and would you consider optimizing the image before pushing to a real production registry?
Answer: One practical consequence of size is slow docker push process. In addition, ECR charges per GB stored per month, so the consequence of a bloated image will be both slow push and reoccuring monthly cost. One way to optimize is to used multi-stage Docker builds technique (compiling/downloading in one stage, copying only the final artifacts into a slim runtime stage).
